---
import LegalLayout from "../../layouts/LegalLayout.astro";

const subprocessors = [
  {
    name: "[Cloudflare, Inc.]",
    purpose: "Website hosting, CDN, and DNS",
    data: "Server logs — IP address, user agent, path, timestamp",
    location: "[Global edge; US entity]",
  },
  {
    name: "[Email provider]",
    purpose: "Transactional email — licence file delivery, renewal notices",
    data: "Name, work email address, organisation",
    location: "[TBD]",
  },
  {
    name: "[Stripe, Inc.]",
    purpose: "Payment processing and invoicing",
    data: "Billing contact, payment metadata. Card details go to the processor and never to us.",
    location: "[US / Ireland]",
  },
  {
    name: "[Support desk / CRM]",
    purpose: "Support tickets and sales correspondence",
    data: "Correspondence and contact details",
    location: "[TBD]",
  },
  {
    name: "[Diagnostics store]",
    purpose: "Storing opt-in support diagnostics",
    data: "Installation identifier, licence identifier, version, operational counters. Never domains, certificates, or findings.",
    location: "[TBD]",
  },
];
---

<LegalLayout
  title="Subprocessors"
  description="Every third party that touches data Merkleye holds, and what each of them is used for."
  updated="[EFFECTIVE DATE]"
>
  <p>
    This page lists every third party that processes personal data on our
    behalf. It is referenced by the
    <a href="/legal/dpa/">Data Processing Addendum</a>, and we give at least
    <span class="tbd">[30]</span> days&rsquo; notice before adding or replacing
    an entry.
  </p>

  <blockquote>
    <p>
      None of these subprocessors receives your monitored domains,
      certificates, or findings. Merkleye is self-hosted, so that data never
      leaves your infrastructure and there is no chain for it to travel down.
    </p>
  </blockquote>

  <h2>Current subprocessors</h2>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Subprocessor</th>
        <th>Purpose</th>
        <th>Data</th>
        <th>Location</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      {
        subprocessors.map((s) => (
          <tr>
            <td>
              <span class="tbd">{s.name}</span>
            </td>
            <td>{s.purpose}</td>
            <td>{s.data}</td>
            <td>
              <span class="tbd">{s.location}</span>
            </td>
          </tr>
        ))
      }
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>Not subprocessors</h2>
  <p>
    Two components ship inside Merkleye&rsquo;s container images and are
    sometimes mistaken for subprocessors on a questionnaire. They are not: both
    run entirely inside your own deployment, under your control, and send us
    nothing.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>certspotter</strong> (SSLMate, MPL-2.0) — tails public
      Certificate Transparency logs from your infrastructure.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>dnstwist</strong> (Apache-2.0) — generates lookalike domain
      permutations locally.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Your deployment does make outbound requests to public CT log operators and,
    optionally, to SSLMate&rsquo;s API if you enable the backfill plugin. Those
    are your deployment&rsquo;s relationships, not ours, and are documented in
    the operations guide.
  </p>

  <h2>Change notifications</h2>
  <p>
    To be notified when this page changes, email
    <a href="mailto:legal@merkleye.com">legal@merkleye.com</a> and ask to be
    added to the subprocessor notice list.
  </p>
</LegalLayout>
